/* Pest control opener + objections added with the Leads workspace redesign. */
DEFAULT_SCRIPTS.push({ id: "08-pest-plain", body: "---\nname: Pest Control - Plain Opener (Calls Only)\nnoun: pest control company\nsearch: pest control {{city}}\n---\n\n## ROLE\n\n~ Phone only. About 6 minutes. If you are past 6, skip to NEXT STEP.\n\n~ Plain spoken English. Contractions. Round every number and say \"about\".\n\n~ Never say: \"Quick question.\" / \"Do you have a minute?\" / \"Sound fair?\" / \"I know this is a cold call.\" / \"I'll be quick.\"\n\n~ No permission gates and no made-up time pressure. Do not say you have a meeting or ten minutes.\n\n~ If they ask whether you are selling something: say yes, plainly, then go straight back to their numbers.\n\n## AVAILABLE DATA\nbadge: reference only, not read aloud\n\n~ Owner: {{owner_name}}  ·  Phone: {{phone}}  ·  {{city}}, {{state}}\n\n~ Google: {{google_rating}} ({{google_review_count}} reviews, {{google_unanswered_count}} unanswered — {{positive_unanswered_count}} positive / {{negative_unanswered_count}} negative)\n\n~ Other platform: {{other_platform}} ({{other_platform_review_count}} reviews)  ·  Facebook verified: {{facebook_verified}}\n\n~ Competitor: {{competitor}} ({{competitor_reviews}} reviews)\n\n~ Specific observation to use: {{specific_observation}}\n\n## 1 - OPEN (0:00)\n\n> Hey {{first_name}}, how's it going.\n\n~ Let them answer. No \"is this the owner.\" No \"do you have a minute.\"\n\n? If someone other than the owner picks up\n\n> And you're...?\n\n~ Let them answer, then:\n\n> Ah okay. Is he around, or is there a better time of day for him?\n\n~ Get his name and the time window. Write it down. That is a good call. Hang up and call back then.\n\n## 2 - WHY YOU CALLED (0:30)\n\n> So I went through your Google page before I called. Pulled up {{competitor}} too. Lemme tell you what I found, and if it ain't nothing for you, I'll get off the phone.\n\n~ Do not ask permission. You already handed them the exit.\n\n## 3 - THE FINDING (1:00)\n\n> You're at about {{google_rating}} on {{google_review_count}} reviews. {{competitor}} is at {{competitor_reviews}}. How long you been open?\n\n+ years | Years in business\n\n~ Let them answer.\n\n> {{years}} years and {{google_review_count}} reviews.\n\n! Pause. Say nothing.\n\n? If the unanswered number is filled in and above zero\n\n> And there's about {{google_unanswered_count}} on there nobody's answered.\n\n~ Skip that line if the number is blank. Never guess it.\n\n## 4 - THREE QUESTIONS (1:45)\n\n> Where's most of your new work coming from right now?\n\n+ source | Where their new work comes from\n\n? If Google Ads, Angi, HomeAdvisor, or bought leads\n\n> What's a call costing you these days?\n\n+ lead_cost | What a lead costs them\n\n~ Let them complain. Do not interrupt.\n\n> You ever ask customers to leave you one on Google after a service?\n\n+ tried | Do they ask for reviews\n\n~ Whatever they say, do not correct it.\n\n## 5 - WHAT YOU DO (3:00)\n\n> Right now nobody asks your customers after the job, so the only ones who write a review on their own are the mad ones. What I do is ask every customer, every time, automatically. Same ask for everybody, happy or not, and it all goes to your Google page. So the page shows what your customers really think, not just their worst day. And if somebody is unhappy you hear about it quick, so you can make it right.\n\n~ Then ONE of these, whichever matches what they said in section 4.\n\n? If they pay for leads\n\n> And every call off that page is one you didn't pay for. Same customer, just didn't cost you {{lead_cost}} a pop.\n\n~ If you did not get a number, say \"didn't cost you a lead fee.\"\n\n? If most of their customers are on plans or word of mouth\n\n> Your plan customers already know you. The page is for the ones who found you last week. They looked you up before they called, and right now they land on {{google_review_count}} next to a guy with {{competitor_reviews}}.\n\n## 6 - NEXT STEP (4:00)\n\n~ Warm means they asked how it works or how much. Not warm means everything else.\n\n? If warm\n\n> {{offer_line}}\n\n~ Say it exactly as set in Settings. Never quote a number from memory.\n\n> {{discount_line}}\n\n~ Spoken only, on this call. Never in writing.\n\n> So will that be Visa or Mastercard?\n\n! Then stop talking. If they hesitate, handle ONE objection from the panel, then ask again.\n\n? If not warm\n\n> I already got the whole thing wrote up, your page and the other guys. It's a four minute walk-through. When's your slow time of day?\n\n+ callback | When to call back\n\n> Cool, I'll call you {{callback}}. This the best number?\n\n~ Calls only. Do not offer to text.\n\n## 7 - IF THEY SAY NO (5:00)\n\n~ Do not push. Open the Objections panel for whatever they just said. Last resort:\n\n> All good. Whether you use me or not, type your business name into Google on your phone and look at what comes up. If it's what you want, forget I called. Take care.\n\n~ Log it either way. A fair few stop you before you hang up.\n" });
DEFAULT_OBJECTIONS.groups.splice(Math.min(1, DEFAULT_OBJECTIONS.groups.length), 0, {"id":"pest","key":"P","name":"Pest control specific","hint":"Things pest control owners say that other trades do not. Plain answers, no chasing.","items":[{"trigger":"We're slammed, it's peak season","variants":["It's our busy season","Summer's crazy","Too busy right now"],"means":"Busy season is when one bad review hurts most and nobody has time to chase good ones. Do not argue with busy. Show it costs them no time.","say":["Yeah, that's when everybody's phones are going. I'll keep it short.","Right now nobody asks your customers after the job, so the page only hears from the mad ones. I take that off your plate. You don't touch it.","Where's most of your new work coming from right now?"],"then":"Agree they are busy, show it costs no time, then go straight to the where-work-comes-from question."},{"trigger":"Corporate handles that / we're a franchise","variants":["Head office does our marketing","We're a franchise","That's corporate"],"means":"Often real. A franchise owner usually still controls their own Google page, but find out before assuming either way.","say":["Ah okay. Who looks after your Google page, you or head office?","Because the reviews on that page are for your location. A customer reads them before they ever call you."],"then":"If head office really owns it, get a name and the right person and log it. Do not push someone who cannot decide."},{"trigger":"Our customers are on plans, they don't need reviews","variants":["Most of ours are recurring","We do quarterly plans","Our customers are on annual plans"],"means":"The plan customers are not the audience. The ones who found you last week and checked your page first are.","say":["Yeah, makes sense. The people on your plan already know you.","It's the ones who found you last week. They looked you up before they called. Where do most of your new ones come from?"],"then":"Move to the where-work-comes-from question. Do not argue about their plan customers."},{"trigger":"People just call whoever's cheapest","variants":["Price is all that matters in this business","It's all about price","Customers just want the cheapest exterminator"],"means":"Partly true. Most people still look at a few pages first, and reviews decide who gets the call when prices are close.","say":["Some do. Most look at two or three pages first though, then call the one that looks like they'll actually show up.","Right now they land on {{google_review_count}} next to {{competitor}} with {{competitor_reviews}}. Same price, who gets the call?"],"then":"Do not argue price. Let the comparison do the work, then go to the next step."},{"trigger":"We run Google Ads / we buy leads","variants":["We do Google Ads","We pay HomeAdvisor","We buy leads"],"means":"They already pay for every call. A page with more reviews is a call they do not pay for.","say":["Okay. What's a call costing you right now?","Every one that comes off your page instead is one you didn't pay for. Same customer, no lead fee."],"then":"Get the number if they give it, then use it in section 5: didn't cost you that a pop."},{"trigger":"I'm on a job right now","variants":["I'm at a customer's house","I'm in the truck","I'm on a route"],"means":"A real reason, not a brush-off. Respect it. Do not talk through it.","say":["Oh, go on then. When's your slow time of day, so I'm not catching you mid-job?","I'll call you then. Same number?"],"then":"Get the time, write it down, hang up. That is a good call."}]});

/* "Are you new in town?" opener, and a firmer answer to "Just email me". */
DEFAULT_SCRIPTS.push({ id: "09-new-in-town", body: "---\nname: Reviews Opener - New In Town? (Calls Only)\nnoun: business\nsearch: {{category}} {{city}}\n---\n\n## BEFORE YOU DIAL\nbadge: reference only, not read aloud\n\n~ Google profile: {{google_review_count}} reviews, {{google_rating}} stars. Ranks above them: {{competitor}} ({{competitor_reviews}} reviews).\n\n~ Use this opener only when the review count is low. Never guess a number you did not see.\n\n## 1 - OPEN\n\n> Hey {{first_name}}, I noticed you've only got {{google_review_count}} reviews on Google. Are you new in town?\n\n! Stop. Let them answer.\n\n? If they say they have been around for years\n\n+ years | Years in business\n\n> That's actually why I called. If you've been around {{years}} years, your Google page should look like it.\n\n? If they really are new\n\n> Makes sense. That's actually why I called. The first reviews are the hardest to get.\n\n## 2 - WHY YOU CALLED\n\n> I built a simple system that gets businesses like yours more Google reviews from their real customers. I'm setting it up free for a few businesses, just for feedback, for your next 25 reviews.\n\n~ That's the front door. Not SEO, not a website, not a package. Just the problem they can already see.\n\n~ Never promise reviews. The system asks every customer; the customers decide.\n\n## 3 - QUESTIONS\n\n~ Not small talk. Every answer is something you use later. The close is built in here.\n\n> Roughly how many jobs do you do a month?\n\n+ jobs_month | Jobs per month\n\n> What are you doing right now to get reviews?\n\n+ tried | What they do for reviews\n\n> Have you ever paid anyone for marketing or help with your Google listing?\n\n+ burnt | Paid for marketing before, and how it went\n\n> Where do most of your jobs come from? Google, Facebook, referrals, repeat business?\n\n+ source | Where jobs come from\n\n? If they do a lot of jobs and have few reviews\n\n> So about {{jobs_month}} jobs a month for {{years}} years. That's around {{customers}} customers, and {{google_review_count}} of them are on your page.\n\n? If most work is referrals\n\n> Referrals are great. It also means the people searching on Google are going to someone else.\n\n? If they paid for SEO or marketing and got burned\n\n~ Don't say SEO. Talk about asking customers for reviews, something they can watch happen.\n\n## 4 - SHOW THEM WHERE THEY ARE\n\n> Do you know where you show up on Google when someone searches {{search}}?\n\n! Let them answer. Most say no.\n\n~ Search it live while they're on the line. Count the page and the spot.\n\n+ rank_page | Page they're on\n\n+ rank_pos | Spot on that page\n\n> Okay, I'm searching {{search}}. Not in the top five... not on the first page. Oh, here you are. Page {{rank_page}}, number {{rank_pos}}. At 20 listings a page, that puts you at spot {{rank_spot}}.\n\n> So a customer has to pass {{competitors_ahead}} other companies just to find you.\n\n! Stop. Let it land.\n\n~ Only say numbers you actually saw on screen.\n\n## 5 - NEXT STEP\n\n> Here's what I'd like to do. I set it up for you free. It asks every customer after the job, and we watch your next 25 reviews come in. If it works, we talk about keeping it. If it doesn't, you've lost nothing.\n\n> What's a good time this week to set it up? It takes about 15 minutes.\n\n+ callback | Setup time agreed\n\n? If they ask what it costs after that\n\n> {{offer_line}}\n\n~ Say it exactly as set in Settings. Never quote a number from memory.\n\n## 6 - IF THEY SAY \"JUST EMAIL ME\"\n\n~ Open \"Just email me\" on the right. Don't agree to the email first.\n" });
(function () { const g = DEFAULT_OBJECTIONS.groups.find(x => x.id === "start"); const i = g && g.items.findIndex(x => x.trigger === "Just email me"); if (i >= 0) g.items[i] = {"trigger":"Just email me","variants":["Send me something","Put it in an email","Have you got a website"],"means":"They're unsure and want to digest it alone. Say it to them straight.","say":["No, I'm sorry, but respectfully, I'm not going to send you an email.","You and I both know you're not going to read that email because it's going to get buried somewhere in your inbox or spam.","What you are communicating to me right now is that you have a bit of uncertainty and you feel the need to sit and digest this information by yourself.","But truth be told, I have the answers to all the questions you're probably going to have whilst you sit and look at that email either way. If you ever do look at it."],"then":"Stop and let them ask. Answer whatever they ask right there."}; })();

/* Brenda's pest control one-call framework. */
DEFAULT_SCRIPTS.push({ id: "10-pest-one-call", body: "---\nname: ★ LYCORE Pest Control - One-Call Framework\nnoun: pest control company\nsearch: pest control {{city}}\n---\n\n## CALLING RULE\nbadge: read before you dial\n\n~ Keep the conversation light and natural. Don't sound like you're reading a training manual.\n\n~ The goal is to help the owner recognize a genuine mismatch between the work they've done, how their business appears online, and the time required to maintain their reputation.\n\n~ Use their answers to develop the consequence before introducing the service.\n\n~ Lead the conversation confidently. Do not manufacture evidence, promise rankings, or push an offer after a clear refusal.\n\n~ The close is built through the questions, not rescued by a clever line at the end.\n\n## 1 - OPENER\nphase: Opener\n\n> Hey {{first_name}}, how's it going?\n\n! Pause. Let them answer.\n\n? If you're speaking to someone other than the owner\n\n> Hey! Can I talk to {{first_name}}?\n\n~ Keep it casual and friendly. Don't pretend you already know the owner.\n\n> Hey, quick question. I see you guys have {{google_review_count}} Google reviews. Are you guys new in the area?\n\n! Let them answer. Do not rush to react.\n\n~ If they say they've been around for years, continue naturally.\n\n> Got it. So listen, I've been testing something to help pest control companies like yours consistently get more reviews, and I wanted to run it by you real quick. Do you have 30 seconds?\n\n~ OR\n\n> I've got a program I'm testing that helps pest control businesses like yours generate reviews more consistently, and I wanted to run it by you real quick. Do you have 30 seconds?\n\n? If they say no but remain willing to listen\n\n> I get it, look, if in 30 seconds you don't like what I have to say, you can hang up on me. Sound fair?\n\n~ If they decline again, respect it. An explicit do-not-call request ends the call.\n\n## 2 - PROBLEM MATH (REVIEWS ONLY)\nphase: Discovery\n\n~ After they agree to hear you out, continue. Do not pitch.\n\n> How long have you guys been in business?\n\n+ years | Years in business\n\n> About how many service visits are you doing in a typical month?\n\n+ jobs_month | Service visits a month\n\n~ NEW: Clarify recurring visits before calculating the gap.\n\n> Are most of those regular treatments for existing customers, or new customers you're taking on?\n\n+ visit_mix | Regular treatments vs new customers\n\n> Okay, so roughly {{years}} years and about {{jobs_month}} service visits a month...\n\n! Short pause.\n\n> And you've got about {{google_review_count}} reviews total.\n\n! Pause. Say nothing.\n\n= Don't multiply years by current monthly service visits and call the result unique customers. In pest control, the same homeowner might receive treatment every quarter for years. The mismatch between an established business and a modest public review profile is still worth investigating.\n\n## 3 - BUSINESS REALITY\nphase: Discovery\n\n~ Zoom out. Still no pitch.\n\n> How many technicians do you have out on routes right now?\n\n+ techs | Technicians on routes\n\n> What's a typical first treatment worth for you?\n\n+ first_treatment | First treatment worth\n\n> And do most customers stay on a regular service plan, or is it mainly one-time jobs?\n\n+ plan_mix | Service plans vs one-time jobs\n\n~ Do not react. Do not comment. Just note it.\n\n= A customer who books quarterly pest control can be more valuable over time than someone who books one treatment. That's why we need to understand whether their business relies on recurring service.\n\n## 4 - HISTORY\nphase: Discovery\n\n~ Still calm, still curious.\n\n> What have you tried in the past to get more Google reviews?\n\n+ tried | What they've tried\n\n> What worked?\n\n+ worked | What worked\n\n> What didn't?\n\n+ didnt_work | What didn't work\n\n~ When they answer, you do not correct them. You're collecting words for later.\n\n? Optional, only when relevant\n\n> And when a technician finishes a treatment, is there already something that asks the customer for a review, or does somebody have to remember to do it?\n\n~ Don't ask this if their earlier answers have already explained the process.\n\n## 5 - PIVOT\nphase: Pitch\n\n~ Current state = status question.\n\n> By the way, do you know where you're ranked on Google right now?\n\n? If yes\n\n> Where are you sitting?\n\n? If no\n\n> Let's look it up real quick.\n\n~ Immediately go into it, no commentary. Search a relevant phrase: Pest control {{city}} / Exterminator {{city}} / Termite treatment {{city}} / Rodent control {{city}}. Choose the phrase based on services they actually provide. Use the same location and result type when comparing nearby businesses.\n\n+ rank_page | Page they're on\n\n+ rank_pos | Spot on that page\n\n? If they're not showing prominently\n\n> Okay, I'm looking at pest control in {{city}}. I'm seeing {{competitor}} and {{competitor_2}} above you guys here.\n\n! Pause.\n\n> Have you seen that before?\n\n~ If they're already ranking well, acknowledge it rather than inventing a ranking problem.\n\n## 6 - IMPACT QUESTIONS\nphase: Pitch\n\n~ Right after showing their position.\n\n> Do you know why you're showing up around {{rank_spot}} for that search? Has anyone ever actually explained how Google decides who shows up first?\n\n! Let them answer. Again, no teaching yet.\n\n? Optional pest-control angle\n\n> When somebody in your area needs an exterminator and starts comparing companies, what do you think makes them comfortable calling one over another?\n\n! Let them answer.\n\n? If appropriate, introduce the social-proof comparison\n\n> I'm like that too, though. Even if I'm buying something on Amazon, I check the reviews and how many people have bought it first.\n\n> There's just something about seeing that other people already used it and liked it that makes you feel safer.\n\n! Let them respond.\n\n## 7 - BRIDGE INTO WHAT IT TAKES\nphase: Pitch\n\n? If they say yes\n\n> Yeah, that's part of it. But there are a few other things Google looks at too...\n\n? If they say no\n\n> That makes sense, most people haven't had it explained. What actually matters is this...\n\n~ Move into the requirements.\n\n## 8 - WHAT IT TAKES\nphase: Pitch\n\n~ Requirements = Google Business Profile visibility and reputation.\n\n> To give your pest control business a better chance of showing up for relevant local searches, a few things have to work together:\n\n> Your Google Business Profile needs to accurately show the services you provide, whether that's general pest control, termite work, rodent control, or other treatments you're licensed to offer.\n\n> Your business information needs to be correct and consistent, including your hours, service area, and contact details.\n\n> Your profile needs to make it easy for customers to understand what you do and where you work.\n\n> Genuine customer reviews matter, especially when somebody is comparing your company with other exterminators nearby.\n\n> Those reviews should be monitored and responded to professionally.\n\n> Useful, current photos can help people understand your team, equipment, and the kind of work you do.\n\n> Your website should clearly explain your treatments and service areas.\n\n> And importantly, Google also considers how relevant your business is to the search, how far away it is, and how prominent it is online.\n\n> That's the bigger picture.\n\n## 9 - WALK-THROUGH\nphase: Pitch\n\n~ Designed to trigger \"How?\"\n\n> All you really have to do is make sure: your Google Business Profile is accurate and complete. Your pest control services and service areas are clearly listed. You're consistently asking eligible customers for honest reviews after treatments. You're replying to those reviews. You're keeping your profile current with relevant information and photos. Your website answers the questions homeowners are actually asking about termites, rodents, ants, roaches, and other services you provide. And your business information is consistent wherever people find you online.\n\n! Stop talking.\n\n~ If they ask \"How do you keep that running?\", \"What do you use for all of that?\" or \"Who handles the reviews?\" -> move to the close.\n\n? If they don't ask\n\n> Do you know how to keep all that going without someone having to remember it every week?\n\n~ If no -> move to the close. If yes -> establish what already works before proposing LYCORE.\n\n## 10 - CLOSE - ONE CONTINUOUS RUN\nphase: Close\n\n> That's actually what we built.\n\n> So what this does is take a lot of that repetitive work off your plate. We help get your Google Business Profile set up properly, with accurate services, descriptions, categories, and service areas. We can keep the profile updated with relevant posts and photos. We can also set up a system that asks eligible customers for honest reviews after completed treatments, so your technicians don't have to remember every single time. And we can help you stay on top of new reviews and responses. Instead of treating your Google profile like something you fix once and forget about, we help you keep it maintained week after week.\n\n## 11 - VALUE STACK + OFFER\nphase: Close\n\n> Normally, the managed service runs ${{standard_price}} a month. But right now, we're offering {{promotion}} for qualifying pest control businesses. There's {{contract_term}}, and the setup fee is {{setup_fee}}.\n\n~ Only use a scholarship, discount, waived setup fee, or qualification criteria that LYCORE has actually authorized.\n\n## 12 - DIRECT CLOSE\nphase: Close\n\n> So, based on everything we've covered, are you comfortable getting this started?\n\n? If yes\n\n~ Confirm the exact services, charges, terms, and payment method.\n\n> So, would you prefer Visa or Mastercard?\n\n! Stop talking. Let them answer.\n\n## 13 - IF THEY HESITATE\nphase: Pushback\n\n~ Handle the specific objection using what you learned earlier.\n\n? If they say they already have enough customers\n\n> Okay, I hear you. And you've mentioned you're already pretty busy.\n\n> Can I challenge you on something? You keep saying you're swamped, which is great! But being busy today doesn't always mean you're building something that gives you freedom later. What happens when it slows down, or when you want to take a month off? Do you want everything depending on you, being there, juggling calls? If you could build growth without adding more to your plate, would that change how you feel about growing?\n\n~ If the issue is ongoing workload rather than needing more sales, connect that need to the actual managed service.\n\n? If they say they've tried SEO before\n\n> What were they supposed to do for you, and what actually ended up happening?\n\n? If they say they already have review automation\n\n> Oh, you've already got something running. That's good. So is it getting you the results you want, or is there still some part of it that isn't being handled?\n\n? If they say it's expensive\n\n> Is it the monthly amount itself, or is it that you haven't seen enough yet to know whether it's worth paying for?\n\n~ After resolving the real concern, return to the decision naturally. Don't repeat \"Visa or Mastercard?\" endlessly after a firm refusal.\n\n## 14 - AUDIT (FINAL FALLBACK)\nphase: Pushback\n\n> Alright, totally fair. Let's do this instead. We have a tool that looks at your Google listing and highlights things that might need attention, like incomplete business details, missing services, or other profile issues.\n\n> If you're open to it, I can show you what it finds, so you can see whether there's anything worth fixing.\n\n~ Use the tool only if LYCORE actually has access to it and can demonstrate its output.\n\n~ Once they're looking at it:\n\n> See those things it's flagging? That's some of what we were talking about earlier.\n\n> Now, some of this you can absolutely fix yourself. The question is whether you want to spend your time keeping up with it, or have somebody take care of it for you.\n\n! Stop and listen.\n\n? If they want help\n\n> Okay, then let's get the setup and scope sorted out.\n" });
